  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "After Max calls Chef Barinov right next to him to ask for permission to be late, Nastya's intimidating father Stepan Andreevich visits Kostya at the bar to ask about wedding plans.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Конечно можно, пойдём опохмелиться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, а у Насти сегодня выходной.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я вижу, что у неё выходной.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я пришёл именно к тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда я вас внимательно слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Это, конечно, не моё дело.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ты на Насте жениться собираешься?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно, мы обязательно поженимся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "И когда же это будет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вообще-то мы сейчас на свадьбу копим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А у меня попросить нельзя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы хотим сделать всё сами.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Now let's go back through their conversation line by line and unpack the Russian phrasing.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "chef",
      "t": "Конечно можно, пойдём опохмелиться.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course you can, let us go cure our hangover.' The grammar packs 'of course' and 'to cure a hangover' into a tight, idiomatic kitchen exchange.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Здравствуйте, а у Насти сегодня выходной.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Hello, Nastya has a day off today.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'hello' before landing on 'day off'. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я вижу, что у неё выходной.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I can see that she has a day off.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'day off'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Я пришёл именно к тебе.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I came specifically to see you.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'I' before landing on 'to you'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда я вас внимательно слушаю.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then I am listening to you carefully.' Kostya chooses the respectful plural 'you' alongside 'to listen' to keep a polite distance. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Это, конечно, не моё дело.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'This is none of my business, of course.' The meaning hinges on 'this' working together with 'business' in spoken Russian.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "Ты на Насте жениться собираешься?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you planning to marry Nastya?' In Russian, the idea of 'you' flows naturally into 'to intend' without extra filler words.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Конечно, мы обязательно поженимся.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Of course, we will definitely get married.' Spoken Russian keeps this brisk, leaning on 'of course' and finishing on 'we will get married'. the guest answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "И когда же это будет?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And just when will that happen?' the guest anchors the line on 'and', while 'will be' completes the thought.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Вообще-то мы сейчас на свадьбу копим.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Actually, we are saving up for the wedding right now.' Here the Russian phrasing highlights 'actually' before landing on 'we are saving up'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "guest",
      "t": "А у меня попросить нельзя?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And could you not just ask me for it?' Notice how Russian expresses 'and' and 'one must not' with crisp inflection rather than helper words. Kostya answers right away.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Мы хотим сделать всё сами.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We want to do everything ourselves.' Kostya anchors the line on 'we', while 'ourselves' completes the thought. Alright, let's hear the entire conversation one more time from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
